WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.457
The NULL checkpoint reviews one of the most practical lessons in the module.

00:00:04.457 --> 00:00:09.722
NOT IN can behave unexpectedly when NULL appears in the returned set.

00:00:09.722 --> 00:00:15.477
NOT EXISTS tests whether a matching row exists and avoids that classic issue.

00:00:15.477 --> 00:00:18.132
NOT IN is not always wrong.

00:00:18.132 --> 00:00:24.057
The important point is that you must understand the possible NULL values before relying on it.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:28.397
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:28.397 --> 00:00:33.892
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:33.892 --> 00:00:37.537
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:41.547
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:41.547 --> 00:00:48.167
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:52.102
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:00:52.102 --> 00:00:56.800
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
